.Dd September 21, 2026
.Dt TOWER 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm tower
.Nd run a command and restart it when it dies
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl u Ar user
.Op Fl c Ar dir
.Op Fl e Ar dir
.Op Fl x Ar codes
.Op Fl b Ar initial
.Op Fl m Ar max
.Op Fl s Ar stable
.Op Fl r Ar count
.Op Fl w Ar window
.Op Fl t Ar grace
.Op Fl l Cm syslog | stderr
.Op Fl o Cm inherit | syslog
.Op Fl T Ar tag
.Op Fl W Ar file ...
.Op Fl H Cm hup | restart
.Op Fl p Ar interval
.Op Fl -
.Ar command
.Op Ar argument ...
.Nm
.Fl v
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
supervises exactly one
.Ar command :
it starts it, restarts it after it exits with an error, waits longer before
each further restart, and stops restarting when the exit code says so or when
failures pile up.
There is no configuration file and no control socket; everything is given on
the command line, so the rc.d script of the supervised service is its
configuration.
.Pp
The
.Ar command
runs in its own process group, so signals sent by
.Nm
also reach its descendants.
A
.Fl -
ends option processing and is needed when
.Ar command
takes options of its own.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl u Ar user
Run
.Ar command
as
.Ar user ,
given by name or numeric uid, with the primary and supplementary groups
listed for it in the password database.
By default the command inherits the user of
.Nm .
.It Fl c Ar dir
Run
.Ar command
with
.Ar dir
as its working directory.
.It Fl e Ar dir
Change the environment of
.Ar command
by the files in
.Ar dir ,
as
.Xr chpst 8
and
.Xr envdir 8
do: each file sets the variable named after it to the first line of its
content, with trailing spaces and tabs removed and NUL bytes turned into
newlines.
An empty file removes the variable.
Files whose name starts with a dot, and directories, are skipped.
.Ar dir
is read on every start, so a change takes effect with the next restart.
It is read by
.Nm
itself, which keeps its own user;
.Fl u
applies to
.Ar command
only.
The files therefore need to be readable by the user of
.Nm ,
not by
.Ar user .
If it cannot be read,
.Ar command
counts as not startable.
.It Fl x Ar codes
A comma-separated list of exit codes that end supervision.
When
.Ar command
exits with one of them,
.Nm
does not restart it and exits with the same code.
The default is
.Cm 0,1 :
a clean exit and, by convention, a configuration error.
.It Fl b Ar initial
The wait before the first restart.
Each further restart after a short run doubles the wait, up to
.Fl m .
The default is
.Cm 1s .
.It Fl m Ar max
The longest wait before a restart.
The default is
.Cm 1m .
.It Fl s Ar stable
A run at least this long counts as stable: the next restart waits
.Fl b
again instead of doubling the previous wait.
The default is
.Cm 1m .
.It Fl r Ar count
Give up after
.Ar count
restarts within
.Fl w .
.Nm
then exits with the last exit code of
.Ar command .
The default of
.Cm 0
never gives up.
.It Fl w Ar window
The window for
.Fl r .
The default is
.Cm 1h .
.It Fl t Ar grace
When stopping, the time between
.Dv SIGTERM
and
.Dv SIGKILL .
The default is
.Cm 5s .
.It Fl l Cm syslog | stderr
Where
.Nm
writes its own messages: to
.Xr syslogd 8
with facility
.Dv daemon
and priority
.Dv notice ,
or to standard error.
The default is
.Cm syslog .
.It Fl o Cm inherit | syslog
Where the output of
.Ar command
goes.
With
.Cm inherit
it is the standard output and standard error of
.Nm ;
what happens to it is up to the caller.
With
.Cm syslog
.Nm
reads both and writes them line by line to
.Xr syslogd 8
with facility
.Dv daemon
and priority
.Dv info ,
in the order they were written.
Empty lines are dropped, lines longer than 4096 bytes are split, and an
unfinished last line is written when
.Ar command
exits.
The default is
.Cm inherit .
.It Fl T Ar tag
The tag of all messages sent to
.Xr syslogd 8 ,
by
.Fl l
and by
.Fl o .
With the name of the service as
.Ar tag ,
restarts and the output of
.Ar command
are found under one name, and several instances can be told apart.
The default is
.Cm tower .
.It Fl W Ar file
Watch
.Ar file ,
for example a TLS certificate or its key, and act as set by
.Fl H
when it changes.
May be given several times; the files must exist when
.Nm
starts.
See
.Sx WATCHING FILES .
.It Fl H Cm hup | restart
What to do when a
.Fl W
file has changed:
.Cm hup
sends
.Dv SIGHUP
to
.Ar command
itself, not to its process group, as
.Xr rcctl 8
does for a reload: for services whose master process reloads its
certificates on it;
.Cm restart
stops
.Ar command
as on
.Dv SIGTERM
and starts it again right away.
The default is
.Cm restart .
.It Fl p Ar interval
How often the
.Fl W
files are checked.
The default is 1m.
.It Fl v
Print the version and exit.
.El
.Pp
Durations take the form accepted by Go, for example
.Cm 500ms ,
.Cm 30s ,
.Cm 2m
or
.Cm 1h30m .
.Sh WATCHING FILES
.Nm
checks the
.Fl W
files every
.Fl p
interval.
A file counts as changed when its device, inode, size or modification
time differ from the last check, so a file replaced with
.Xr rename 2 ,
as ACME clients install certificates, is noticed as well as one rewritten
in place.
Symbolic links are followed.
.Pp
A change takes effect only once a check finds no further change and every
watched file exists.
A certificate and its key that are replaced one after the other thus cause
one reload, after both, as long as the second follows within one interval;
the service never sees a certificate without its new key.
The delay between a change and the reload is one to two intervals.
.Pp
A restart for a changed file is not a failure: it happens without a wait,
does not count against
.Fl r ,
and resets the wait before the next restart to
.Fl b .
A change while
.Ar command
is not running is ignored, since the next start reads the new files
anyway.
.Pp
The checks use
.Xr stat 2
only, without
.Xr kqueue 2
or inotify, because a file changed through another path, such as by the host
of a jail, need not emit events on a
.Xr nullfs 5
mount.
Mount the directory holding the files, not the single files: a file
mount stays bound to the old file after a
.Xr rename 2 .
.Sh SIGNALS
.Bl -tag -width SIGTERM
.It Dv SIGTERM , Dv SIGINT
Stop:
.Nm
sends
.Dv SIGTERM
to the process group of
.Ar command ,
waits up to
.Fl t ,
sends
.Dv SIGKILL
if needed, and exits with status 0.
A stop that arrives while
.Nm
is waiting for a restart cancels the restart.
.It Dv SIGHUP
Passed on to the process group of
.Ar command ,
for services that reload their configuration on it.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
exits with 0 after a stop by signal; with the exit code of
.Ar command
when it stops restarting, either because the code is listed in
.Fl x
or because
.Fl r
was reached; with 127 when
.Ar command
cannot be started; and with 2 on invalid arguments.
.Pp
A
.Ar command
killed by a signal is treated as having exited with 128 plus the signal
number, as in the shell, and is therefore restarted unless that number is
listed in
.Fl x .
.Sh EXAMPLES
Try it in the foreground with a command that always fails:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ tower -l stderr -b 1s -m 10s -r 3 -w 1m -- sh -c 'echo hi; exit 4'
.Ed
.Pp
An
.Ox
rc.d script for a service that exits with 1 on a configuration error and
with other codes on lost connections:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
#!/bin/ksh
daemon="/usr/local/sbin/tower"
daemon_flags="-- /usr/local/bin/e3dc-mqtt -config /etc/e3dc-mqtt.toml"
\&. /etc/rc.d/rc.subr
rc_bg=YES
rc_reload=YES
rc_cmd $1
.Ed
.Pp
On
.Fx ,
let
.Xr daemon 8
detach and write the pid file, and let
.Nm
do the restarting:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
command="/usr/sbin/daemon"
command_args="-f -p ${pidfile} /usr/local/sbin/tower -- /usr/local/bin/e3dc-mqtt"
procname="/usr/local/sbin/tower"
.Ed
.Pp
In a
.Fx
jail that gets its certificates read-only through
.Xr nullfs 5
from the host, reload dovecot when the host has installed new ones:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
tower -T dovecot -o syslog -H hup \e
    -W /usr/local/etc/certs/fullchain.pem \e
    -W /usr/local/etc/certs/privkey.pem \e
    -- /usr/local/sbin/dovecot -F
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr chpst 8 ,
.Xr daemon 8 ,
.Xr envdir 8 ,
.Xr rc.d 8 ,
.Xr rcctl 8 ,
.Xr syslogd 8
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Markus Teichmann Aq Mt markus.teichmann@go-21.de
.Pp
The name comes from the I/O tower in the film
.Em Tron ,
the place where programs get back in touch.
.Sh CAVEATS
As long as
.Nm
runs, the service looks alive to
.Xr rcctl 8
and
.Xr service 8 ,
even while
.Ar command
is dead and waiting for its next restart.
Set
.Fl r
where that would be misleading, and read the log.
